  <running_head>DISSERT. HISTOR. POLIT.</running_head>
  <main_text>
    <l n="1" para="true">Platones &amp; Aristoteles, desiderarit. Cer-</l>
    <l n="2">tarunt cum Cicerone Latina Oratores</l>
    <l n="3">facundia &amp; ad ipsam Virgiliani Carmi-</l>
    <l n="4">nis Majestatem Poëtæ assurrexerunt.</l>
    <l n="5" para="true">28. Unum hoc deplorandum quod</l>
    <l n="6">jam non sensim &amp; per gradus, sed impetu</l>
    <l n="7">in antiquam cuncta prolabantur barba-</l>
    <l n="8">riem, sato an segnitia juventutis, quæ</l>
    <l n="9">crida ex Schola Academiæ studia infe-</l>
    <l n="10">reris, &amp; vix degustato humanitatis initio,</l>
    <l n="11">festinatis honoribus in Ecclesiam ac</l>
    <l n="12">Remp. ingenti temeritate, nec minori</l>
    <l n="13">damno propellit.</l>
    <l n="14" type="heading" level="1" caput="32" para="true">DISSERTATIO XXXII.</l>
    <l n="15" type="heading" level="2" para="true">Specimen Historiæ Americanæ Veteris</l>
    <l n="16" type="heading" level="2">ante adventum Hispanorum.</l>
    <l n="17" para="true">CUm Jofuam fugientes Chananæi, to-</l>
    <l n="18">tam Africam, qua mediterraneo ma-</l>
    <l n="19">ri adjacet, multitudine sua inundassent,</l>
    <l n="20">ab extrema Mauritaniæ ora in Atlanti-</l>
    <l n="21">cum mare longius provecti, primo ad for-</l>
    <l n="22">tunatas &amp; Cassiterides insulas, deinde ul-</l>
    <l n="23">terius in oppositam continentem vene-</l>
    <l n="24">runt. Ubi &amp; regionis amoenitate, &amp; jac-</l>
    <l n="25">tura navium suarum, &amp; Oceani interce-</l>
    <l n="26">dentis sævitia moti, accedentibus forte &amp;</l>
    <l n="27">aliis causis, vastas terras &amp; illum orbem,</l>
    <l n="28">sive primi incolæ, sive aliis qui jam eo</l>
    <l n="29">delati</l>
  </main_text>
